00:05:18.071 --> 00:05:21.815
But we also have another
guest joining us today.

00:05:21.815 --> 00:05:25.315
So I'm gonna bring him
on here in a moment.

00:05:25.315 --> 00:05:27.600
Hey, Ben, how's it going?

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.190
- Hello there, good to be here.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.860
- So Ben is here on our team

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.063
and also our director of education,

00:05:35.063 --> 00:05:38.790
but Ben leads an incredible
team of evangelists

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.440
and community members and
community leaders here at Adobe.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.270
And Ben, you're quite the
legend in your experience

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.043
in education, including
your tenure at Adobe.

00:05:48.043 --> 00:05:51.050
So can you share a little
bit more about yourself

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.739
and your background?

00:05:52.739 --> 00:05:56.940
- Sure, not sure about
legend, but thank you.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.600
Yeah, I have a wonderful
team here at Adobe.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.400
We focus on helping
K12 educators worldwide

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.598
inject creativity in the
classroom in very meaningful ways.

00:06:06.598 --> 00:06:08.190
I am an educator myself,

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.890
I've been teaching for over 30 years.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.180
I'm also an author, I've
written 40 plus books

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.670
that have taught coding
to over a million people.

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:18.980
And actually more relevance
to our topic today,

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.960
my most recent book use game coding

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.570
to introduce creative thinking
and creative problem solving

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.537
for middle and high school students.

00:06:26.537 --> 00:06:29.501
So that's what we do, we
spend a lot of time thinking

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:31.050
about creativity and how
to help K12 teachers.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.600
And it's a pleasure to be here.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.820
- Thank you for joining us, Ben and Sal.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.780
I know we have a great
conversation ahead of us,

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.040
in particular, focusing
on mastery learning

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.793
and creative confidence in the classroom.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.980
But Sal, in particular for Khan Academy,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.630
how do you see educators in the community

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:58.913
cultivating a creative
confidence in their students?

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.560
- Well, you know, I think
creativity can only happen

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.546
when there's space for it.

00:07:07.546 --> 00:07:11.323
And so, I think it's important.

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:14.370
I think about this as a parent as well.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.360
You know, when I was a kid,

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.490
my family didn't have a lot of resources,

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:21.080
and so like, I was bored a lot,
but that boredom, you know,

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:22.970
I was like, okay, there's some
pencil, there's some paper,

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:25.590
I guess I'm gonna do something,
I used to draw a lot.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:28.880
And you know, at the time I
remember I used to envy the kids

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.830
who could go to these fancy summer camps

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:35.770
and do this and do that, and
those things can be very nice.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.810
But I now realize in my own childhood,

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.150
there was something nice about that,

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:43.090
boredom fostering creativity,
and making you learn

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:45.970
to make things as opposed to
kind of being directed always

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:47.230
on what you should do.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.380
So I think about this as a parent now,

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.000
'cause I now do have the resources

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.760
and my kids probably are
getting overscheduled.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.610
And I'm like, "Oh, am I
squeezing out the time

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.020
for some of their creativity?"

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:59.600
And I also sometimes in classrooms,

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.687
I think teachers can feel
a in on them to like,

00:08:02.687 --> 00:08:06.230
"Hey, I gotta make sure that
every second of this classroom

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:07.560
is productive for my students.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.150
And that's a good instinct,
although sometimes I think

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:15.370
it is nice to create,
you need some guardrails

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.590
for the students and
some objectives for them,

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:19.240
but then give them a little bit of space,

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.015
give them a little bit of
space to potentially fail,

00:08:22.015 --> 00:08:26.420
to have a larger variance
in what they might produce.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:29.060
And I think those are the
areas where you can let

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.720
that creativity, which I
think all human beings have.

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:35.310
But a can atrophy if it's
not given enough space

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:36.143
and enough time.

00:08:36.143 --> 00:08:38.247
And obviously it can
happen at every level.

00:08:38.247 --> 00:08:41.038
You know, a lot of people
think of creativity,

00:08:41.038 --> 00:08:42.570
they think of an artist splashing paint

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:43.920
on canvas or something like that.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.040
That's obviously one form of creativity.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.740
But creativity can even happen

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.000
when you're looking at an equation.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.490
And kind of the non-creative form

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:57.267
of processing the equation is,

00:08:57.267 --> 00:08:59.939
"All right, I just plug numbers into it,

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:00.772
and it gives me an answer."

00:09:00.772 --> 00:09:02.160
The creative form is like,
"What does this mean?

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.280
How does this connect to other things?

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.530
Can I draw connections with it?

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.570
Can I manipulate it to
have it express in new ways

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:09.940
that tell me other
things about the universe

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:12.153
that it can connect to other ideas?"

00:09:12.153 --> 00:09:13.140
So, I think it can happen in anything,

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.223
but you need some time and space for it.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:19.820
- Absolutely, I'm seeing
here in the chat too,

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:24.130
educators really resonating
with their own experiences.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.270
And Sally, I love how you mentioned

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.510
how creativity is not just the arts.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:30.970
And I think that's
sometimes a misconception

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:32.540
that we see in schools,

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.650
but it's that whole making connections.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.070
And what is the creative thinking
process look like in math,

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:41.473
look like in English
language, arts and sciences?

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.760
One of the things that
we see in our community,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.970
and I know in Khan Academy as well,

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.690
is that their educators from
all different subject areas.

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:52.700
So as you said, just providing that space

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:54.630
and that opportunity for students

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.873
to have that freedom is so powerful.

00:09:57.873 --> 00:10:01.919
And, Ben, anything to
add to that in terms of

00:10:01.919 --> 00:10:04.856
what you've been seeing at Adobe?

00:10:04.856 --> 00:10:09.560
- Yeah, so I echo everything Sal said,

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:11.730
we need to reach students and educators.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.190
And when it comes to reaching students,

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:18.281
honestly, not much as needed,
it's get out of the way,

00:10:18.281 --> 00:10:20.090
give them room to play,

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.610
give them the latitude
to experiment and fail,

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:26.130
and give them license to
iterate, permission to discover,

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:29.300
find their own voice, that's
actually the easy part.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:32.600
The trickier part we find
are the teachers themselves.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.490
They are overwhelmed, they
have often strict guidelines

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:37.570
and constraints that
they have to adhere to.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:39.980
And the last thing we ever
wanna do is give them more work,

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:43.538
that's unrealistic, it's
also frankly disrespectful.

00:10:43.538 --> 00:10:46.540
And so we spend a lot of
time working with educators

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:48.359
to help them understand

00:10:48.359 --> 00:10:50.870
how to meaningfully inject
creativity in every subject,

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:53.020
as you just noted in a very relevant way.

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.400
You know, we have the tools,

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.863
we think we have the best
tools for them to be using.

00:10:56.863 --> 00:10:59.540
We have great content on
our education exchange,

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:01.970
including all the materials created

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:03.530
in partnership at Khan Academy.

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:06.350
And so we work with school
districts and leads,

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:09.920
with trainers, and our focus
is to help them understand

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:14.920
the how, how to be less
prescriptive, which Sal alluded to,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.210
how to rethink assignments,
how to rethink the balance

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.357
of lecturing verses doing,

00:11:20.357 --> 00:11:21.770
how to rethink students taking agency

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.280
in finding their own path?

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.900
And we do this with
training, with mentoring,

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.660
with professional development,

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:31.679
participation in our Adobe
Creative Educator community.

00:11:31.679 --> 00:11:34.060
And all of these, the
goal of all these programs

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.062
is really to empower
educators with the confidence

00:11:37.062 --> 00:11:40.893
to let the students do what
they naturally do best.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.950
- And I know part of that,
Ben, as you mentioned,

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:47.560
are the tools, and I know we'll dive

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:50.590
into some of the tools that are offered

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.625
and possible for educators later.

00:11:52.625 --> 00:11:54.550
But absolutely, and kind of looking

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.740
at across all subject areas,
providing the freedom,

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:01.279
providing the space
for them to be creative

00:12:01.279 --> 00:12:03.730
is again so powerful.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:07.468
And I'm seeing some
comments in the chat here.

00:12:07.468 --> 00:12:09.460
Tim says, "I love giving students

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:11.900
and myself some free time and space,

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:14.440
boredom can be a terrific breeding ground

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:16.530
for creativity and imagination."

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:21.470
And so I think really
brought that well together.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.960
And I see Kuben, nice
to see you joining us

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.610
from Johannesburg in South Africa.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.067
And Marilyn also says,

00:12:29.067 --> 00:12:32.192
"Appreciate the sensitivity
in regards to teachers."

00:12:32.192 --> 00:12:35.193
So thank you they're from Marilyn.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.200
But I know we've touched
on this a little bit

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:41.550
in your introduction, Sal.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:46.210
But when it comes to thinking
about creative confidence

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:51.210
and then an overlap between
mastery mindset and creativity,

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:55.690
how do those all come into play together?

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:58.780
And especially during April,
during testing season,

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.660
how can educators really approach

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.150
these three pieces together,

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:05.060
of mastery, mindset and creativity?

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.552
- Yeah, and I'll add another dimension

00:13:07.552 --> 00:13:09.195
of growth mindset to that.

00:13:09.195 --> 00:13:10.820
You know, it's very in Vogue these days

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:11.940
to talk about the growth mindset,

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:13.480
there's obviously a lot of
great research from folks

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:17.820
like Carol Dweck and Angela
Duckworth and other folks.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:19.852
And it's all on this principle that like,

00:13:19.852 --> 00:13:22.830
a growth mindset is better,

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:27.480
and it's a mindset in which
you don't stigmatize failure,

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:30.130
you realize that if you didn't succeed,

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:31.820
that's actually, and if you reflect on it,

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:33.330
that's actually where
you're gonna grow the most.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.250
And if you have a stick intuitiveness,

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:37.810
a greater perseverance
and you keep working on it

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.770
so that you can get to a
point of fluency or mastery,

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.040
that that is how you're
going to improve the most

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:45.353
in almost in any domain.

00:13:45.353 --> 00:13:49.170
Mastery learning is how
that gets implemented

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:51.720
in an academic setting
in my point of view.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:54.660
As I hinted at, in a traditional model,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:58.800
a student gets an 80% on
a test, and they kind of,

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:00.520
you market that down in their grade book,

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.180
and the student says, I
guess I'm a C student.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:07.860
And one that probably creates
a fixed mindset on one level,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.775
'cause you're not even
telling the student,

00:14:09.775 --> 00:14:10.930
"Hey, why don't you
give another shot at it,

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:11.763
get better at it.

00:14:11.763 --> 00:14:13.670
This isn't a bad thing that
you got some questions wrong,

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:15.730
those are opportunities to learn."

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:17.980
But it also, then those
gaps persist, which is,

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.210
I think particularly
harmful in STEM subjects,

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:21.500
I think is harmful in all subjects.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:26.500
But if you only understood how
to divide decimals 80% well,

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:30.090
now all of a sudden the whole
classroom is doing equations

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:33.010
with decimals or doing some more,

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.040
decimal, dividing decimal word problems.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.300
How are you going to really
be able to master that

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:42.300
if you couldn't understand
the more fundamental idea.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:47.510
And so I think, mastery,
which is in my definition is,

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:49.170
always having the opportunity incentive

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.426
to improve your state.

00:14:51.426 --> 00:14:53.266
I think it's important for developing

00:14:53.266 --> 00:14:58.266
the tools so that you can
express yourself creatively.

00:14:59.187 --> 00:15:02.340
You know, I think if you look
at the field of mathematics,

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:07.240
the fields of science, these
are clearly creative fields.

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:09.140
You're trying to come up with new ways

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:11.800
of describing reality, new
ways of understand reality,

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:15.171
new ways of engineering, new
ways of inventing things.

00:15:15.171 --> 00:15:18.290
And I think the reason why
sometimes there's a stereotype

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:22.000
that it's not, is because
so much time and effort

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:24.650
goes behind building the competencies

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:27.040
so that you can be creative in the space.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:29.660
It's kind of like if we
were trying to be artists,

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:31.190
but you know, for the first 12 years

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:32.850
you're just learning to mix paint,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.680
you'll never get to be the artist,

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:36.060
or you're learning to be a dancer,

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.090
but for the first 12
years you're stretching,

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:41.810
and you never get to really
express yourself through dance.

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:46.810
And so I'm a big fan of,
mastery learning's gonna build

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:49.380
those competencies so
you feel that confidence

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:51.880
so that when you do engage on,

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:56.020
Hey, why does the Krebs cycle
work this way in biology?

00:15:56.020 --> 00:16:00.750
Or, what are these different forms

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:01.730
of an equation really tell you?

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:03.930
you need that fluency in
order to be able to dig

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:06.690
into the conversation, but then it unlocks

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:09.520
an incredibly creative one.

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:11.450
And the more that we can bring

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.110
some of what real math petition do,

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:17.680
what real scientists do into
the classroom, all the better.

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:21.600
But I will say, historically,
there's been a schism

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.390
with educators where
there's been what you call

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:24.410
the progressive educators.

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:26.230
That's like, "Yes, let's bring this all in

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:28.460
to the classroom tomorrow or right now

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:29.920
where kids are acting as scientists,

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:32.432
acting as mathematicians,
acting as historians."

00:16:32.432 --> 00:16:34.307
And then you have of your
traditionalists who say,

00:16:34.307 --> 00:16:36.400
"Well, no, no, the kids really
need to have their fluency,

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:38.813
really need to be able to solve
certain types of problems."

00:16:38.813 --> 00:16:40.541
I generally think it's both.

00:16:40.541 --> 00:16:45.390
You really can't activate a
lot of the creative, you can't,

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:47.150
you know, the scientists who
are thinking like scientists

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.536
have a lot of content knowledge
and have a lot of fluency.

00:16:49.536 --> 00:16:51.950
And if they don't, they're
gonna have trouble thinking

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:53.650
like a scientist in a lot of cases.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:56.640
So, can you use mastery learning,
tools like Khan Academy,

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.940
other tools, to get that foundation,

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.740
and then hopefully free
up time for more of

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.190
the creative aspects,
which exist in every field.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:10.050
- Absolutely, and that
whole process of iteration

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.740
and having a growth mindset and continuing

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:14.900
to fail along the way is something

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:17.910
that we've seen just even within design

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:22.910
and creating different visual
concepts and communicating.

00:17:23.036 --> 00:17:25.890
So I know it's something
within our community,

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:28.400
we've seen quite a bit as well.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.020
And, Ben, from the Adobe side,

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:33.660
anything to add to that
in terms of mastery,

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.800
mastery learning and the growth mindset?

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:39.730
- Yeah, I'll add to that,
obviously creativity

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:41.650
is a foundational skill, right?

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:44.347
It's about agency, it's
about finding your own voice,

00:17:44.347 --> 00:17:46.240
it's about iteration.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:49.530
And nurturing that creative thinking,

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:51.480
nurturing creative problem solving

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:53.290
and creative covers in general,

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:55.960
those are critical to
developing a growth mindset

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:57.890
at your own pace, which
is literally mastery.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:01.610
You know, I'm a huge fan of
Sir. Ken Robinson's definition

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:03.550
that creativity is the process

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:05.830
of having original ideas that have value.

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:07.790
And in that definition, the
world that's most important

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:10.530
to me is original, the unique, the idea

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:12.640
that we're encouraging a
child to find their own path.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:14.900
That isn't necessarily exactly the same

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:17.490
as the kids sitting next to
him or her in the classroom.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:20.250
And this is real and
meaningful and impactful.

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:22.210
And it has recognized implications

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:23.890
both short term and long term, right?

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:26.720
There's lots of research
that shows the impact,

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:29.986
short term impact on education
up comes in the classroom,

00:18:29.986 --> 00:18:32.950
but it has long term
implications for future success.

00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:37.180
Look, I don't want education
to ever be solely driven

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:38.780
by jobs and hiring trends, right?

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:40.460
We don't wanna be, you know,

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:42.130
in first grade worrying
about what occupation

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:43.680
you'll have 20 years down the line,

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.263
we want well-rounded students.

00:18:45.263 --> 00:18:48.790
But at the same time, we can't
ignore future opportunities,

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:50.770
and industry doesn't
want more of the same.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:53.360
You never hear a company
saying, "I love this employee,

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:55.960
I want 1000 more exactly the
same as him or her," right?

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:58.530
We want diversity, we want
it not just for diversity,

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:01.490
but also diverse thoughts,
diverse problem solving,

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:03.050
diverse thinking, diverse backgrounds,

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:04.950
diverse voices, that's critical.

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:06.550
And we have to nurture that.

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:09.080
And if we fail to encourage that unique,

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:11.330
honestly we are being
kids of their future.

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:13.697
And for us, that's what creativity

00:19:13.697 --> 00:19:15.287
in the classroom is all about,

00:19:15.287 --> 00:19:17.393
and that creativity must be nurtured.

00:19:17.393 --> 00:19:20.797
And the truth is, tying back
to where we started off,

00:19:20.797 --> 00:19:24.323
nurturing creativity is
truly encouraging mastery.

00:19:27.964 --> 00:19:30.620
- Absolutely, and I know
that speaking of jobs

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.020
of the future, we don't know

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:34.140
what a lot of those physicians will be,

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:37.190
but what we do know is if
you have a growth mindset,

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:39.710
you can think creatively, you
can make those connections,

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:41.460
you can iterate along the way,

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.150
approach problems from different angles,

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:44.730
that's what's going to be successful,

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:47.440
and ultimately what
employers are looking for.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:51.240
So, and going quickly
